00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.330 align:start position:0%
 
you  what happened at the beginning of  everything this question has preoccupied  scientists and philosophers for  centuries but now new data from a  telescope in Antarctica may be giving us  the answer the confirmation that  physicists have long been looking for  that the universe rapidly expanded right  after the Big Bang a theory called  cosmic inflation physicists believed  that in the first billionth of a  trillionth of a trillionth of a  trillionth of a second of the universe's  life it underwent a mind-bogglingly  rapid expansion but finding evidence for  this is fiendishly hard researchers must  look in the very fabric of space-time  for clues according to Einstein when  something really violent happens like  the rapid expansion of the universe  after the Big Bang it creates ripples in  space-time he called these ripples  gravitational waves as gravitational  waves passed through the universe they  compress and expand space finding the  very first gravitational waves would  tell us about the birth of the universe  if only we could see them the expanding  universe would have stretched these  primordial waves so much that today the  distortions they caused would be  absolutely miniscule so how on earth or  in space are we supposed to find them  imagine a beach the waves roll in and as  they do they churn up the sand mixing  and distorting it the waves recede with  the tide but they leave behind an  imprint ripples in the sand the universe  isn't covered in sand in fact it's  mostly nothing but one thing it is full  of is radiation the cosmic microwave  background the cosmic microwave  background is the afterglow of the Big  Bang and it is everywhere  perhaps this radiation could hold an  imprint of gravitational waves to find  out a team led by the Center for  Astrophysics in Massachusetts built  super-sensitive radiation detectors they  took them to the South Pole installed  them in a telescope and started looking  for ripples in the sand nine years of  painstaking search and analysis later  and they found something swirling  patterns in the cosmic microwave  background created they say by  gravitational waves from the beginning  of the universe this is the most direct  evidence ever for the existence of  gravitational waves as predicted by  general relativity almost a hundred  years ago but more than that it is the  most convincing evidence yet for  inflation it seems that finally  scientists can claim to understand the  very earliest moments of the universe  [Music]  [Music]  you

